ct with the incumbent's advantages", "Killin's $5M fundraising and a strong Democratic national environment keep it close", "Polymarket prices Crank's chances near 75%"], "rationale": "Crank holds the advantages of incumbency in a Republican-leaning seat, and nearly every expert rating plus the prediction market point his way. Killin's record fundraising and a favorable national environment make this competitive, but not enough to overcome the district's Republican lean.", "watch": "Fresh public polling or a late surge of outside spending behind Killin could push the race into true tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 90 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Jeff Crank (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Colorado's 5th District, anchored in Colorado Springs, has favored Republicans by about five points in recent elections, and Representative Jeff Crank is seeking re-election. Jessica Killin, a Democrat, has run an unusually well-funded challenge, raising nearly five million dollars and keeping the race on the national map. Nine of ten expert ratings call the contest Lean Republican, one calls it Likely Republican, and a single outlier favors Killin.\n\nThe two public polls, both from a Democratic firm, show Crank ahead by one and three points, though the newest dates to July. Prediction markets give Crank roughly a three-in-four chance of winning. Killin's fundraising edge and a national environment tilted toward Democrats make this closer than a typical Colorado Springs race, but incumbency and the district's lean still favor Crank by a few points.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.332, "margin": -2.57}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.3239, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.3532, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.346, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2907, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.3013, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09